> 1. Minetown

> I often end up at minetown via trap door, without
> my pet. Trying to keep pacifist conduct w/o a pet
> is insane.

Not insane, just a little harder.

Following in your pet's footsteps is a good way to
avoid Mines' many traps. Searching as you go is a
bit dicier, for risk of starvation, but it also
works. If you are the kind who can stand to play
obsessively slowly, marking on graph paper or with a
drawing tool on a screen-shot of the dungeon level
every square on which your pet steps is one way to
find safe places to walk. The disadvantage is that
the game doesn't show you where your pet steps in
the possibly several steps it takes between your
turns, for long enough for you to see them, so you
only see a subset of the "proven safe" squares.

"Herding" your pet ahead of you down narrow
corridors improves, but doesn't remove, the problem
of pets going missing when you need them to defend
you. Dancing circles around your pet while monsters
fight your pet and pursue you gives you a chance to
evade the monsters, get your pet to kill the
monsters, and possibly grab the corpse away from
your pet before your pet eats a nourishing corpse.

Don't be afraid to stand next to your pet and take a
few hits if your HP suffice, while staying in
grabbing distance of where the corpse will fall. Run
in circles only as needed. The monster will split
its attention between you and your pet, so you both:
give yourself a chance to grab the corpse, and:
decrease your pet's chance of being killed.

Taming _lots_ of pets is a _fine_ idea; they tramp
out the safe squares for you faster, kill the
monsters cooperatively, but they annoy you to
distraction too. Learn to cope.

> I've attempted to cloister the monsters by writing
> Elbereths in the dust in the corridors, but the
> monsters don't *seem* to respect E unless I'm
> standing on it.

They respect it if _anything_ is standing on it, but
not if nothing is, so dropping a zorkmid, a stray
trash weapon, or a rock, will keep hostile monsters
off the E squares.

Unfortunately, peaceful monsters will ignore
Elbereth (and may wipe out the dust engraving, I'm
not sure), pets will too (but will mostly respect a
square on which you've dropped a _cursed_ object,
n.b.).

> Furthermore, if I'm in deep trouble, the Minetown
> watch seem to clog corridors needed for escape.

Yep, so when you're in trouble in Mines Town, stick
as much as possible to open spaces. Keeping your pet
with you, displacing it to be on the side of you
where the attacking monster is, helps a lot.

Learn to recognize the several Mines Town layouts
(or use the dungeon gazetteer spoiler), so you know
right away which way to go to the temple or to the
up stairs, in case you fall into Mines Town rather
than walking into Mines Town.

> 2. Getting enough loot / divine protection (aka
> 2000 zorkmids and exp 1, for 9 units protection
> guarantee)

> Arcs don't spellcast and can't heal themselves, so
> surviving long enough to accumulate 2000 Zorkmids
> from vaults/gems requires extreme caution.

Don't forget credit cloning, a tool every protection
runner should learn to use skillfully. Mine Town
shops will _easily_ provide $3000 to $16,000 if you
bankrupt all the shopkeepers (even Izchak) instead
of killing them, and you can usually find one or
more shops in the main dungeon before going into the
Mines.

The entire rocky part of the dungeon levels and of
the mines levels are capable of holding treasure,
and the archeologist has that lovely starting
pickaxe, so while you have food, just digging out
levels for treasure works really well. The deeper
you go, the richer the random gold piles get, too,
and the archeologist has the huge advantage of
knowing which of the gems are good ones, and having
a sack in which to stash them so they don't clutter
inventory.

I split about half my protection runs to go via the
Sokoban treasure zoo, which can be done at XL:1
fairly easily if you drag along a couple of buff
pets. That's another fairly good source of wealth.

The moral here is that there's no _requirement_ that
a protection run be done at flank speed. Slow and
easy is a whole different, but also workable,
protection run approach.  You just need to work hard
on your evasion and pet herding skills to be
successful at a slow-mode protection run.

> Pits, E squares, etc. And *no healing* except
> loafing on dust- written E-squares in early game.

That's mostly useless, just run away to heal.

> 3. Keeping pet close by

> Essential for a protection run, but my pets
> unfortunately have other plans, such as attacking
> floating eyes, blue jellies and watch captains,
> investigating rock traps, and wandering off. The
> Arc's intrinsic speed doesn't help this.

It is more useful than you'd think to work the odds,
interposing your body at least one of the directions
you don't want your pet to go. Herding your pet
ahead of you down corridors is very helpful.
Carrying a tripe ration is a pet magnet for
carnivorous pets, increasing the proportion of the
time they move toward you, I'm not sure which treat
works that way for vegetarian pets.

